WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.040
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 33. It's May 9, 2026.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:13.380
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise, no jargon. Let's go.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.640
So imagine you have a friend who knows your entire schedule, your step count from last night,

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:25.740
and even notes from your last checkup. That friend could spot when you are running low on energy

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.220
and suggests a quick break that actually fits between classes.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.600
Google just added something like that inside their Google Health app, powered by their Gemini model.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.380
Instead of generic advice like everyone should walk 10,000 steps,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.880
this coach looks at your real data from wearables and fitness apps.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:54.100
It pulls together your medical records, your sleep logs, and your calendar to spot patterns you might miss.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:00.720
Think of it like a smart dashboard in your car that warns you before the fuel light comes on.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.020
Except here it reminds you to drink water or stretch before you feel drained.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:12.320
For students juggling school in sports, that means suggestions that respect busy days

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.180
rather than pushing goals that feel impossible. You might get a note suggesting a short walk

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:24.080
between classes based on how poorly you slept the night before. Right now, the feature is rolling

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.440
out on both Android and iOS, so if you have a Google account, you can open the app,

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:34.920
connect any track as you already use, and start chatting with the coach about your typical day.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:42.220
It is completely free to try, and the best part is it turns all that scattered health data into one

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:50.320
helpful conversation. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the Deep Dive, where we look under

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:56.680
the hood at how these models actually work. You know how when you are texting a friend, your phone

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:02.460
guesses the next word before you finish typing? Now picture that same auto-complete looking ahead

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.500
and guessing the next three or four words all at once instead of one at a time.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.940
That is basically what multi-token prediction does inside newer models, like Gemma 4.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:20.160
By planning several steps ahead, the model finishes its answers faster without losing quality.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.780
It still checks its own work, but it spends less time going back and forth on every single word.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:32.220
Google reports this trick can speed up certain workflows by up to three times.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:39.080
So next time someone mentions multi-token prediction, you can picture it as smarter auto-complete

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:44.740
that thinks a few words ahead, not so scary once you see the simple idea behind it.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.680
And that is basically how multi-token prediction works.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:54.920
Let's move to some cool stuff you can try right now with tools that are already free.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.800
First up, you can turn any random object into a clean, labeled diagram, perfect for school projects

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:09.380
or just curiosity. ChatGPT's image generator now creates educational cross-section drawings even

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:15.900
when you start with nothing but a description. The trick is asking it to restore an imaginary object

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.180
into a scientific illustration with neat labels and pointer lines.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.680
Go to the chatGPT website, start a new chat, choose the create image option,

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:33.800
and try this exact prompt. Restore an imaginary bicycle into a clean educational cross-section

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.180
on a white background, preserve the most distinctive visible features,

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:44.240
and add neat labels with pointer lines. Watch it build layers and parts you can screenshot

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:50.420
straight into your notes. Try it with something everyday like a backpack or a phone charger,

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.060
and you will end up with a diagram that looks like it came from a textbook.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:01.560
Next, head over to Reddit to see how realistic AI images have become and train your own eye.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:07.300
People are posting AI-generated photos that look almost identical to real ones,

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:13.160
and the comments turn into a game of spotting what is fake. It is a fun way to understand

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:20.340
what current image models can and cannot do yet. Visit Reddit.com and search for the phrase

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:26.160
levels of realism in the image generation. Then scroll through the latest examples.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:33.160
Pick one photo and try describing it back to chatGPT or another free image tool to see how

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:39.320
close you can get on your own. You will quickly notice the tiny details that still give AI away,

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:42.560
like lighting or textures that feel slightly off.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:52.080
Now for a couple of quick bits that caught my eye this week. Sony believes faster AI tools will let

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.600
small game development teams create more titles while keeping human artists in charge of the creative

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:03.580
vision. It is a reminder that these tools speed things up but do not replace the people telling the

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:11.620
stories. In other news, Chrome quietly added a 4GB local AI model that runs right on your device

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:18.120
without sending data to the cloud. You can turn the feature off in settings if you prefer not to keep

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:25.160
the extra storage on your computer. That is it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:31.040
where you are right now. If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:37.680
That's literally how learning works. Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:44.720
And if you'd rather watch than listen, find us on YouTube at nera network. Links in the show notes.

